{"study_accession":"PRJNA641887","secondary_study_accession":"SRP268823","title":"Identification of a CD117+​ ​CD71+​ ​ early ​unipotent​ neutrophil progenitor population in human bone marrow","description":"Neutrophils play critical roles in health and disease. Due to their very short half-life in blood and tissue, neutrophils are constantly replenished by bone marrow progenitors. Thus, a comprehensive understanding of bone marrow neutrophil development is of paramount importance to identify how neutrophil production is altered in disease. Recently, two novel human neutrophil progenitor populations were identified; ‘human neutrophil progenitor’ or ‘hNeP’ (Lin-​ ​CD66b+​ ​CD117+​ ​) ​and ‘neutrophil precursor’ or ‘preNeu’ (Lin-​ ​CD66b+​ ​CD15+​ ​CD49d+​ ​). How these subsets fit into the neutrophil lineage is unclear. By using mass and flow cytometry, we show that hNeP are a heterogenous population containing a homogeneous progenitor subset termed ‘early neutrophil progenitor’ or ‘eNeP’ (Lin-​ ​CD66b+​ ​CD117+​ ​CD71+​ ​). ​Surface marker and RNA expression, together with the ability to form colonies ​in vitro and exclusively produce neutrophils ​in vivo in humanized NSG-SGM3 mouse transfer experiments indicate that eNeP are hierarchically the ‘earliest’ cells within preNeu. eNeP constitute ~0.14% of human bone marrow neutrophils, while preNeu constitute ~5% of bone marrow neutrophils. Furthermore, we have identified CD71 as a novel neutrophil surface marker associated with distinct early neutrophil developmental stages. Intriguingly, CD71+​ characterizes proliferating neutrophils, which are expanded in the blood of melanoma and lung cancer patients and detectable in human lung tumors. Collectively, our findings i) identify CD117+​ ​CD71+​ ​eNeP as an early neutrophil progenitor population, ii) introduce a unified model of human neutrophil bone marrow development, iii) identify novel surface markers for distinct neutrophil developmental stages and iv) provide evidence for neutrophil progenitor expansion in cancer. Overall design: Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing for sorted neutrophils cells in human bone marrow","pubmed":"32814027","geo_series":"","n_samples":12,"samples":[{"sample_accession":"SAMN15369439","study_accession":"PRJNA641887","blood_class":"bone_marrow","class_from":"sample","title":"CD16N_CD71N_S9","description":"CD16N_CD71N_S9","sex":"","geo_sample":"GSM4637175","access":"open","dbgap":"","n_runs":1,"first_public":"2020-08-07","attributes":[["source_name","N2"],["disease","Healthy"],["tissue","Bone marrow"],["cell population","CD16- CD71-"]],"runs":[{"run_accession":"SRR12091659","experiment_accession":"SRX8617552","library_layout":"PAIRED","library_selection":"cDNA","instrument_model":"Illumina HiSeq 2500","read_count":42947466,"base_count":4380641532,"first_public":"2020-08-07","experiment_title":"Illumina HiSeq 2500 sequencing: GSM4637175: CD16N_CD71N_S9 Homo sapiens RNA-Seq"}],"labels":[],"single_cell":false},{"sample_accession":"SAMN15369440","study_accession":"PRJNA641887","blood_class":"bone_marrow","class_from":"sample","title":"CD16N_CD71N_S5","description":"CD16N_CD71N_S5","sex":"","geo_sample":"GSM4637174","access":"open","dbgap":"","n_runs":1,"first_public":"2020-08-07","attributes":[["source_name","N2"],["disease","Healthy"],["tissue","Bone marrow"],["cell population","CD16- CD71-"]],"runs":[{"run_accession":"SRR12091658","experiment_accession":"SRX8617551","library_layout":"PAIRED","library_selection":"cDNA","instrument_model":"Illumina HiSeq 2500","read_count":95957053,"base_count":9787619406,"first_public":"2020-08-07","experiment_title":"Illumina HiSeq 2500 sequencing: GSM4637174: CD16N_CD71N_S5 Homo sapiens RNA-Seq"}],"labels":[],"single_cell":false},{"sample_accession":"SAMN15369441","study_accession":"PRJNA641887","blood_class":"bone_marrow","class_from":"sample","title":"CD16N_CD71N_S2","description":"CD16N_CD71N_S2","sex":"","geo_sample":"GSM4637173","access":"open","dbga